### Actual IR or error
Under ptoas 0.29 the inner-loop `wait_flag(PIPE_MTE1, PIPE_MTE2, EVENT_ID4)` and
`EVENT_ID6` before TLOAD are dropped, and the four trailing waits before TPUSH
are also dropped. The lead-in/lead-out EVENT_IDs are reshuffled and no longer
re-establish the broken invariant. The bad inner loop looks like:

```cpp
for (size_t v49 = v21; v49 < ((size_t) v11); v49 += v21) {
  int32_t v50 = (int32_t) ((uint32_t) ((int32_t) v49) * (uint32_t) v12);
  int32_t v51 = (int32_t) ((uint32_t) v50 + (uint32_t) v12);
  /* ... build v52 ... */
  TLOAD(v52, v55);                              // missing EVENT_ID4 wait
  set_flag(PIPE_MTE2, PIPE_MTE1, EVENT_ID4);
  /* ... build v56 ... */
  TLOAD(v56, v59);                              // missing EVENT_ID6 wait
  set_flag(PIPE_MTE2, PIPE_MTE1, EVENT_ID5);
  /* ... */
};
// post-loop: only set_flag's; the 4 wait_flag(MTE1,MTE2,4..7) are gone
```

Effect: the next iteration's GM→L1 `TLOAD` can overwrite the L1 Mat tile while
the previous `TMOV(L1→L0A/L0B)` is still reading it. The cube unit then issues
`TMATMUL_ACC` on partially-overwritten L0A/L0B operands and the accumulated
result drifts. End-to-end this manifests as a Qwen3-32B precision failure that
disappears when the kernels are rebuilt with ptoas 0.27.

The same pattern appears identically in `scope3_incore_5_aic.cpp` and the
matching `scope3_incore_{0,5}_aiv.cpp`. Other kernels in the same build
(`gate_proj`, `up_proj`, `scope3_incore_4`, `post_rmsnorm`) are byte-identical
between 0.27 and 0.29 — the regression is specific to the K-loop pipelined GEMM
shape used in `scope3_incore_{0,5}`.

